adjudication.rationale:
In packages/core/EventManager.ts the diff changes destinationCalendar to an array and introduces:

```ts
const [mainHostDestinationCalendar] = evt.destinationCalendar ?? [];
if (evt.location === MeetLocationType && mainHostDestinationCalendar.integration !== "google_calendar")
```

If `evt.destinationCalendar` is `[]` (explicitly used elsewhere in this diff as the “no calendar” sentinel, e.g. bookingReminder.ts and webhook.e2e.ts) or `null/undefined`, destructuring yields `mainHostDestinationCalendar === undefined`. When `evt.location === MeetLocationType`, the condition evaluates the RHS and dereferences `mainHostDestinationCalendar.integration`, which will throw `TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined`. This breaks the exact fallback path it is meant to implement (Google Meet selected without a Google Calendar).

The diff changes `destinationCalendar` to an array and updates the fallback logic in `packages/core/EventManager.ts` to:
```ts
const [mainHostDestinationCalendar] = evt.destinationCalendar ?? [];
if (evt.location === MeetLocationType && mainHostDestinationCalendar.integration !== "google_calendar") {
  evt["location"] = "integrations:daily";
}
```
If `evt.destinationCalendar` is `null`/`undefined`/`[]`, destructuring yields `mainHostDestinationCalendar === undefined`, and `mainHostDestinationCalendar.integration` will throw a `TypeError`. This is a real runtime bug introduced by the diff (the previous code used optional chaining: `evt.destinationCalendar?.integration`). The condition is explicitly meant to handle the case where Google Meet is selected without a Google Calendar, i.e., where `destinationCalendar` may be missing/empty, making the crash scenario plausible.
